    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we can use the Binomial Theorem. The Binomial Theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(x+y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nFor our specific case, \\(n = 6\\). We need to find the term where the power of \\(x\\) is 2 and the power of \\(y\\) is 4. This corresponds to the term where \\(k = 4\\).\n\nAccording to the Binomial Theorem, the general term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{k} x^{6-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nWe need \\(k = 4\\) because it is the term we are looking for. Substituting \\(k = 4\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} x^{6-4} y^4 = \\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\binom{6}{2} = \\frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!} = \\frac{6!}{2! \\cdot 4!}\n\\]\n\nUsing the values of factorial, we get:\n\n\\[\n6! = 720, \\quad 2! = 2, \\quad 4! = 24\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\frac{720}{2 \\cdot 24} = \\frac{720}{48} = 15\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
